> Deriving the two-pixel split

After exchanging axes for a steep line and ordering endpoints, the major
coordinate increases and the absolute minor-axis slope is at most one.
For a major-axis centre \(i\), the ideal minor coordinate is
\(y(i)=y_0+m(i-x_0)\). Write \(j=\lfloor y(i)\rfloor\) and
\(f=y(i)-j\). If the column's total ink weight is \(w_i\), require
both conservation of that weight and the correct weighted centre:

$$
\alpha+\beta=w_i,\qquad j\alpha+(j+1)\beta=w_i y(i)
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\alpha=w_i(1-f),\qquad\beta=w_i f.
$$
\alttext(Two adjacent pixel weights sum to the column weight, and their weighted vertical position equals that weight times the ideal line position. Solving gives one minus the fractional part and the fractional part, both multiplied by the column weight.)

For an interior column, \(w_i=1\). Nonnegative weights follow from
\(0\le f<1\). The first-moment identity explains the split more
precisely than saying one pixel is always the nearer one: the upper pixel
is nearer when \(f>1/2\). The weights form a linear, triangular filter
in the minor direction. They are not the exact intersection areas of a
finite-width geometric stroke with square pixels.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/wu_split.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto pixels = dm::algorithms::xiaolin_wu_line(0,0,12,5);
  std::print("{}\n",dm::algorithms::wu_pixel_table(pixels).head(12).to_md(6));
  auto plot = dm::algorithms::wu_pixel_plot(pixels);
  plot.line({0,12},{0,5},"ideal centreline",dm::RGB{220,38,38},1)
      .title("Two adjacent cells share the ink at each major-axis step")
      .size(1000,630);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");plot.save_svg("build/wu_split.svg");
}
```
