\image(figures/wu_split.svg)
// result_end
\caption(An integer-endpoint line under the existing full-column policy. Darker grey is larger ink weight; the red line shows the ideal centreline. Grey cells show numerical weights without display gamma correction.)
\alttext(A magnified line on a pixel grid with grey cells on both sides of a thin red diagonal centreline. Each interior column splits one unit of ink between adjacent rows.)

> Endpoint conventions change the conservation statement

The four-argument function retains the previous convention: every rounded
major-axis column has weight one, including both endpoints. An integer
segment of major-axis length \(D\) therefore has total weight \(D+1\).
This useful simple convention differs from endpoint-clipped Wu filtering.
The explicit `ClippedColumns` overload instead uses

$$
w_i=\max\left(0,\min(x_1,i+1/2)-\max(x_0,i-1/2)\right),\qquad
\sum_i w_i=x_1-x_0=D.
$$
\alttext(A column weight is the length of the segment's major-axis interval intersecting the pixel-centred unit interval. Because those unit intervals partition the axis, their weights sum to the segment's major-axis length.)

For separated integer endpoints, the first and last columns have weight
one half. The same interval-intersection formula also handles a short
segment whose endpoints round to the same column without counting the
endpoint twice. Nonzero segments have total weight
\(D=\max(|\Delta x|,|\Delta y|)\); this is a projected length, not
Euclidean arclength. For slope magnitude at most one, the latter is
\(D\sqrt{1+m^2}\). Constant ink per major-axis step does not imply
constant total ink per Euclidean length across all orientations.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::print("| segment | policy | major length | total ink | contributions |\n| :--- | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto endpoints : {std::array<double,4>{0,0,12,5}, {0.2,0.3,3.7,1.6}, {0.1,0.2,0.2,0.25}}) {
    for (const auto policy : {dm::algorithms::WuEndpointPolicy::FullColumns,dm::algorithms::WuEndpointPolicy::ClippedColumns}) {
      const auto pixels = dm::algorithms::xiaolin_wu_line(endpoints[0],endpoints[1],endpoints[2],endpoints[3],policy);
      const auto summary = dm::algorithms::wu_pixel_table(pixels) | dm::summarise(dm::agg::sum("weight","total"));
      std::print("| ({},{}) to ({},{}) | {} | {:.3f} | {:.6f} | {} |\n",endpoints[0],endpoints[1],endpoints[2],endpoints[3],
          policy == dm::algorithms::WuEndpointPolicy::FullColumns ? "full" : "clipped",
          std::max(std::abs(endpoints[2]-endpoints[0]),std::abs(endpoints[3]-endpoints[1])),summary.double_at("total",0),pixels.size());
    }
  }
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| segment | policy | major length | total ink | contributions |
| :--- | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| (0,0) to (12,5) | full | 12.000 | 13.000000 | 26 |
| (0,0) to (12,5) | clipped | 12.000 | 12.000000 | 26 |
| (0.2,0.3) to (3.7,1.6) | full | 3.500 | 5.000000 | 10 |
| (0.2,0.3) to (3.7,1.6) | clipped | 3.500 | 3.500000 | 10 |
| (0.1,0.2) to (0.2,0.25) | full | 0.100 | 1.000000 | 2 |
| (0.1,0.2) to (0.2,0.25) | clipped | 0.100 | 0.100000 | 2 |
// result_end
\caption(Total ink under two explicitly named endpoint policies, including fractional and same-column segments.)

A coincident-endpoint call is explicitly a unit-weight point marker in
both policies, rather than a zero-length stroke. Coordinates must be
finite and safely inside the integer range before rounding. Endpoint
reversal and exchanging axes preserve the corresponding raster, subject
to floating-point rounding. The implementation evaluates the line directly
at each centre to avoid accumulated slope-update drift.

For \(P\) major-axis columns, there are at most \(2P\) contributions:
\(O(P)\) time, \(O(1)\) auxiliary state and \(O(P)\) returned storage.
The library's grayscale plotting helper additionally merges repeated
coordinates in \(O(P\log P)\) time. This floating-point implementation
does not reproduce every integer optimisation of Wu's original paper.

> Applying the rasteriser to monthly observations

`wu_polyline()` keeps the frame's row order and scales each axis into the
viewport interior, leaving a one-pixel margin. A constant coordinate maps
to its axis centre. It neither averages observations nor sorts them.
Each adjacent pair of monthly records becomes a segment with clipped
endpoints. Contributions at shared coordinates add; the rendering helper
saturates their sum at one before mapping it to grey. This is additive ink
visualisation, not alpha-over compositing or a model of exact stroke joins.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/wu_airline_rasters.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto air = dm::datasets::air_passengers() | dm::mutate("time", [](const auto& r) {
    return 12*r.get_double("year")+r.get_double("month");
  });
  auto layout = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2);
  std::print("| viewport | input months | contributions | pixel coordinates | summed ink | largest pixel sum |\n"
      "| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto dimensions : {std::array<int,2>{48,16},{96,32}}) {
    const auto pixels = dm::algorithms::wu_polyline(air,"time","passengers",dimensions[0],dimensions[1]);
    const auto table = dm::algorithms::wu_pixel_table(pixels);
    const auto summary = table | dm::summarise(dm::agg::sum("weight","ink"),dm::agg::max("weight","largest"));
    std::print("| {} x {} | {} | {} | {} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} |\n",dimensions[0],dimensions[1],air.nrows(),
        pixels.size(),table.nrows(),summary.double_at("ink",0),summary.double_at("largest",0));
    layout.add(dm::algorithms::wu_pixel_plot(pixels).title(std::format("{} x {} pixels",dimensions[0],dimensions[1])).size(600,315));
  }
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");layout.size(1200,315).save_svg("build/wu_airline_rasters.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| viewport | input months | contributions | pixel coordinates | summed ink | largest pixel sum |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 48 x 16 | 144 | 496 | 172 | 100.182115 | 1.968142 |
| 96 x 32 | 144 | 718 | 402 | 221.133218 | 1.568698 |
\image(figures/wu_airline_rasters.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The same 144 records mapped into 48 by 16 and 96 by 32 pixel viewports. Coordinate counts include zero-weight neighbours; summed ink is measured before display saturation.)
\alttext(Two small raster renderings of the airline time series, with visibly larger cells at 48 by 16 pixels and finer seasonal detail at 96 by 32 pixels.)

At the lower resolution several months share nearby horizontal pixel
columns. Fractional shading softens the staircase but cannot encode each
original measurement distinctly. The numerical input is preserved in the
frame; the rendered image is a lossy representation. Larger viewports also
change the relative horizontal and vertical scaling slightly because of
the fixed margin, so total ink is not required to double exactly.

> Conclusion

Two adjacent weights preserve a column's total ink and its ideal minor-axis
first moment. Explicit endpoint policies distinguish the existing
full-column variant from interval-clipped endpoint filtering. Correct
conservation is per projected major-axis length, and neither brightness nor
grey display values should be mistaken for exact geometric coverage.
The airline series demonstrates practical polyline rendering and the
limits of a small pixel grid: antialiasing improves the representation of
a line without adding missing resolution to the observations.

> References

- X. Wu (1991). *An Efficient Antialiasing Technique*. ACM SIGGRAPH Computer Graphics
  25(4), 143–152. https://doi.org/10.1145/127719.122734
- R datasets documentation, *AirPassengers: Monthly Airline Passenger Numbers 1949–1960*.
  https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/datasets/html/AirPassengers.html
